From: Konstantine Zhurakovskiy <konstantine.zhurakovskiy@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 21:21:44 +0700
Subject: upstream-pr-2344: Ocarina of Time: a texture ID's read mark goes with
 its next texture when the ID has not been read for 600 frames

From seedds/nxnative PR #2344 (commit 4ec0c1c1), taken early for Ocarina of Time; dropped once nxnative lands it. Its message:

A texture ID's read mark goes with its next texture when no shader has read the ID for 600 frames, so area loads late in a session no longer make every streamed texture at once: a read mark (pool_seen) stayed with its ID through every new texture (PR #2022) and was cleared only when the ID was emptied, so the marks grew with every area passed through. An hour into Ocarina of Time (0400486024DEC000, v131072) the pool walk line counted 19,201 of its 33,024 IDs as read (22.7 GB of textures), and a walk makes a marked ID's texture whatever its room (pool_entry, under POOL_REQ_BYTES, 128 MB a frame), so each area load, which streams thousands of textures into IDs earlier areas had read, had them made as fast as the read budget allowed: the loads into Hyrule Field and the passages around it froze in runs of 200-670 ms frames for seconds, with 100-300 first texture uploads a frame and GPU allocated at 9.8 GB against 3.9 GB in the session's first minutes. Now pool_seen_scan takes the GPU's marks back every 60 frames (SEEN_REARM; an exchange, so a mark set meanwhile stays for the next scan) and notes the frame each mark was last found in (g_seen_at, up to 60 frames after the read); the shaders set them again at their next read (dm_pool_mark is unchanged, so the chain is not). mtl_reg_tex keeps the mark for the ID's next texture only when the ID was read within the last DM_NVN_SEEN_KEEP frames (600 by default): the streamed replacement of a material on screen keeps it as PR #2022 needs, and a texture streamed into an ID left behind holds its stand-in until it is read (pool_stand). The marks are scanned in a pass of their own before the walk, which may stop at the read budget. A stale 1 read by work still running just after a take-back skips one mark, which a read after the next take-back sets. DM_NVN_SEEN_KEEP=0 keeps every mark until its ID is emptied, as before; the pool stand-ins line counts the marks kept and dropped. The reference entry (Area loads froze late in a session as read marks piled up) is new, the entry for PR #2022 and Ocarina's note link it. Checked: t_pool_seen_replace (60 checks, 8 new: a read notes its frame, the GPU's mark taken back while the host's stays, a mark not read within the window dropped with the next texture, an ID read again keeps its mark; 52 with DM_NVN_SEEN_KEEP=0), t_pool_standin, t_pool_notes, t_pool_chunks, make run in dm/gfx/tests (every suite passes; the six that import dmsh_side need Python 3.10 or later), test_docs. Two sessions of play on an M5 Pro (48 GB, macOS 26.6.2), not on the same route: without the change, the runs of 200-670 ms frames above after the first hour and tens of 300-frame windows with a p95 above 40 ms; with it, 78 minutes, one such window in 903, GPU allocated 7.2-9.6 GB from the 6th minute on, following the area rather than the time played, and the area loads single frames of 102 and 155 ms (658 and 248 first uploads). 92,703 new textures kept their ID's mark and 46,614 dropped it; stand-ins made 1,235,328 in 6.19 s over the session against about 128,000 without the change (23 us of the submit thread a frame on average). Not run: an A/B on one route, regress.py check (no other game here streams into a bindless pool this way), another game, a Mac with less memory, lint_c.py, iOS. Open: whether the area-load frames of 100-155 ms left come from the stand-ins made in them or from the first uploads. test_env_switches fails on main as here, for DM_NVN_VCONV_KEEP, which this does not touch

Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>
