@@ -321,7 +321,7 @@ The replay on Metal (`dm/gfx/nvn/nvn_mtl.m`) and the Vulkan and OpenGL layers. E
 
 - **Symptom:** large static and mesh-shader models (Ocarina of Time's castle town wall, rocks, trees, Lon Lon Ranch's barn) draw black for exactly one frame, with the fog in front and the terrain untouched, in pairs four or five frames apart; a few times a minute in play, at fixed places of a route.
 - **Cause:** the models' shaders read their textures through the bindless pool table (`pool_gpu`). When a walk writes an ID's entry (`pool_entry`) it makes the ID's Metal texture if it fits the walk's room (`pool_room`, within `pool_budget()`), or, whatever the room, if a shader has read the ID (`pool_seen`, under `POOL_REQ_BYTES`); otherwise the entry is the dummy (black), and `pool_seen_scan` makes the texture after the first read. In Ocarina of Time the room is always 0: `pool_budget()` is a twentieth of Metal's working set (909 MB on a 24 GB Mac) against 15-18 GB of `currentAllocatedSize`, so only read IDs are made ahead. `mtl_reg_tex` cleared the mark whenever the game registered another texture into the ID, so when the game's streaming replaced the 17 textures of a material set at once (3-7 MB each, finalizing the old texture first; each step registered twice, 4-5 frames apart), the replacements counted as unread and their first reads found the dummy, one frame per registration.
-- **Fix:** the read mark is the ID's, not its texture's: it stays while the ID holds a texture and is cleared only when the ID is emptied, so the next walk makes a replacement under the read budget. A render target no pass has written stays the dummy all the same (`pool_unwritten_target`), which is what kept transient targets from being made ahead. A texture streamed into an ID no shader has read yet is not covered by this: its first read finds the ID's stand-in ([next entry](#a-streamed-textures-first-read-drew-its-surface-dark-for-one-frame)).
