+### Area loads froze late in a session as read marks piled up
+
+- **Symptom:** about an hour into a session, walking into an area or through a passage between areas freezes the picture in runs of 200-670 ms frames for seconds, where the same loads were smooth in the session's first minutes; GPU allocated climbs from 3.9 GB to 9.8 GB, and the `[longframe]` lines show 100-300 first texture uploads a frame. No red line.
+- **Cause:** a read mark (`pool_seen`) stayed with its ID through every new texture ([previous entry](#models-black-for-one-frame-when-a-streaming-step-replaces-their-textures)) and was cleared only when the ID was emptied, so the marks grew with every area the player passed through: an hour in, the `pool walk` line counted 19,201 of 33,024 IDs as read, 22.7 GB of textures. A walk makes a marked ID's texture whatever its room (`pool_entry`, under `POOL_REQ_BYTES`, 128 MB a frame), so an area load, which streams thousands of textures into IDs that earlier areas had read, had them made as fast as the read budget allowed instead of leaving them to stand-ins until a shader read them.
+- **Fix:** a mark lasts while its ID is read. `pool_seen_scan` takes the GPU's marks back every 60 frames (`SEEN_REARM`; an exchange, so a mark a shader sets meanwhile stays for the next scan), the shaders set them again at their next read (`dm_pool_mark`, unchanged), and the host notes the frame each mark was last found in (`g_seen_at`, up to 60 frames after the read). `mtl_reg_tex` keeps the mark for the ID's next texture only when the ID was read within the last `DM_NVN_SEEN_KEEP` frames (600 by default, 10 s at 60 fps): the streamed replacement of a material on screen keeps it as before, and a texture streamed into an ID left behind areas ago holds its stand-in until it is read. A stale 1 that work still running reads just after a take-back skips one mark, which a read after the next take-back sets. `DM_NVN_SEEN_KEEP=0` keeps every mark until its ID is emptied, as before. The `pool stand-ins` line counts the marks kept and dropped.
+- **Check:** `t_pool_seen_replace` (a read notes its frame; the GPU's mark taken back while the host's stays; a mark not read within the window dropped with the next texture; an ID read again keeps its mark), also with `DM_NVN_SEEN_KEEP=0`; `t_pool_standin`, `t_pool_notes`, `t_pool_chunks`. Two sessions of play on one Mac (M5 Pro, 48 GB, macOS 26.6.2), not on the same route: without the change, the runs of 200-670 ms frames above at the area loads after the first hour, tens of 300-frame windows with a p95 above 40 ms (up to 380 ms) and GPU allocated at 9.8 GB; with it, 78 minutes, one such window in 903, GPU allocated 7.2-9.6 GB from the 6th minute to the end, following the area rather than the time played, and the area loads single frames of 102 and 155 ms (658 and 248 first uploads, 243 and 277 MB). 92,703 new textures kept their ID's mark and 46,614 dropped it. Stand-ins made: 1,235,328 in 6.19 s over the session, against about 128,000 in the session without the change, 23 µs of the submit thread a frame on average. Not run: an A/B on one route (`DM_NVN_SEEN_KEEP=0` against the default), another game that streams into a bindless pool, a Mac with less memory, the iPhone. Open: whether the area-load frames of 100-155 ms left come from the stand-ins made in them or from the first uploads.
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md) (v131072, Hyrule Field and the passages around it).
